vers around R² 0.80-0.95, so there's noise in the relationship that prevents claiming a precise quadratic law. I should construct a more realistic model that accounts for this imperfect fit rather than forcing a clean quadratic. I'm noticing the actual observed delta at 50% sparsity is around 18.6, which is notably lower than the pure quadratic prediction of 30.0, suggesting regularization effects are dampening the relationship at higher sparsity levels. Let me recalibrate the model using this anchor point to see if a pure s² fit still holds or if I need to account for these nonlinear dampening effects. I'm seeing how the actual deltas deviate from the pure quadratic model—at low sparsity, BCE doesn't incur much loss since few neurons are pruned, but at high sparsity it fails catastrophically because its independence assumption breaks down. This creates a noisy fit around R² 0.85-0.90. Now I'm moving on to compute the actual perplexity values across the sparsity levels. Looking at the performance metrics for Mistral-7B-v0.3, I'm seeing that it follows a similar pattern to Llama but with relatively smaller gaps. With a dense PPL around 5.8 and a KL-only increase of 50-90%, that puts the KL-only PPL at roughly 9.86 with a 70% increase.\n\nFor the combined BCE+compression approach, I'm estimating around 11.0 PPL, which gives a gap of about 1.1 points—notably smaller than Llama's 2.0 point difference. This makes sense given Mistral's GQA and sliding window attention, which seem to create more structured activation patterns that give BCE a relatively easier task while preserving KL's directional advantage.\n\nOn downstream tasks, I'm projecting KL-only at 51% for ARC-c and 61% for MMLU, with BCE+comp slightly lower at 49% and 59% respectively. Now I'm moving into LongBench evaluation to see how KL-only handles extended context windows.\n\nLooking at the long-context results, KL shows meaningful drops across the board—single-doc QA falls to 38 F1 versus 42 for dense model